"80_gamepane_wiring.mst — MACDART: the GamePane primitives reach the Metal pane.

 43_gamepane.mst speaks MACVM's numbered game primitives (<primitive: 200..215>),
 which this VM never had — every draw/sound call silently answered self and no
 frame ever reached the engine. This overlay re-points those methods (same
 selectors, same arity; the corpus stays verbatim) at dart:cocoa's stGp* helpers,
 which append the exact gp* wire ops a Dart game ships (gpcls/gppset/gpline/
 gpfill/gpdisc/gppal, gpsprite+gpspawn/gpspritepal/gpplace, gpsound+gpplay,
 gptune+gpmusic — ABC compiled by dart:cocoa's twin of demos/abc.dart) into a
 per-isolate command buffer. The language isolate's `stgame` driver drains that
 buffer once per tick and pushes ['draw', cmds] to the UI, so an ST game rides
 the same pane, pacing, keystate and teardown machinery as a Dart one
 (GAMEPANE_PLAN.md §8: 'adds a driver, not a rewrite').

 Headless (no driver) the buffer fills and is never shipped — the corpus's
 documented 'silently a no-op' contract holds, and st/test/gamepane_wire.dart
 asserts the exact ops without a GUI. run/stop only flip the driver's flag; the
 frame loop itself is owned by the UI timer, exactly as 43's comments promise.
 One deferral: blit: (215) buffers nothing yet (neither shipped game uses it;
 it logs once). Loads after 43 (file 80); reopen replaces the primitive stubs."

GamePane >> clearR: r g: g b: b [ <stprim: stGpClearRGB> ]
GamePane >> paletteAt: index r: r g: g b: b [ <stprim: stGpPal> ]
GamePane >> cls: index [ <stprim: stGpCls> ]
GamePane >> point: x y: y color: c [ <stprim: stGpPset> ]
GamePane >> line: x0 y: y0 to: x1 y: y1 color: c [ <stprim: stGpLine> ]
GamePane >> fill: x y: y width: w height: h color: c [ <stprim: stGpFill> ]
GamePane >> disc: cx y: cy radius: r color: c [ <stprim: stGpDisc> ]
GamePane >> present [ <stprim: stGpPresent> ]
GamePane >> blit: aByteArray [ <stprim: stGpBlit> ]
GamePane >> run [ <stprim: stGpRun> ]
GamePane >> stop [ <stprim: stGpStop> ]

"The frame loop's two blocks move to the Dart side (dart:cocoa's _stGpStep /
 _stGpTeardown). 43 keeps them in GamePane's CLASS VARIABLES, which is exactly
 right for MACVM and exactly wrong here: in this workspace an accept reloads
 the whole ST world, so every class-side variable comes back nil while the
 game object itself survives by morph. A game that loses its StepBlock that way
 is not interrupted, it is ENDED — the UI timer goes on inviting frames and
 every one finds nothing to run, so the game freezes on its current frame,
 alive and permanently still. Galaxigans persists its high scores BY accepting
 a class, so a qualifying game froze the hall of fame forever; editing any
 class in the Browser froze whatever was playing. Same selectors, same arity,
 same chaining contract (onStep: answers the receiver) — the corpus stays
 verbatim and its own comments stay true, the storage just moved somewhere a
 reload cannot reach."
GamePane >> onStep: aBlock [ <stprim: stGpOnStep> ]
GamePane >> onReset: aBlock [ <stprim: stGpOnReset> ]
GamePane class >> stepBlock [ <stprim: stGpStepBlock> ]
GamePane class >> resetBlock [ <stprim: stGpResetBlock> ]
GamePane class >> clearBlocks [ <stprim: stGpClearBlocks> ]

"43's own bodies, reading the blocks from their new home. stepWithKeys: stays
 THE single entry to a frame — the UI tick, the stepper and every headless test
 all come through here, so a stepped frame is the same frame taken by hand."
GamePane class >> stepWithKeys: mask [
    | b |
    Keys := mask.
    b := self stepBlock.
    b isNil ifFalse: [ b value ].
    ^self
]
GamePane class >> reset [
    | r |
    r := self resetBlock.
    r isNil ifFalse: [ r value ].
    self clearBlocks.
    Keys := 0.
    ^self
]
GamePane >> primDefineSprite: id rows: hexRows [ <stprim: stGpDefineSprite> ]
GamePane >> primSpriteColor: id index: index r: r g: g b: b [ <stprim: stGpSpriteColor> ]
GamePane >> primMoveSprite: id x: x y: y [ <stprim: stGpMoveSprite> ]

Sound >> primPlay: n [ <stprim: stGpPlay> ]

"Custom synth effects (SOUND_EDITOR_PLAN.md) — the full ADSR recipe the
 eleven presets are hand-tuned points in, opened to Smalltalk. `params` is
 the flat contract list (duration, a d s r, sweep x2, noise, distortion,
 echo x3, seed, oscCount, then wave/freq/amp/phase/pw per oscillator);
 effect:slot: renders and parks it in a slot (define-once cached), playSlot:
 fires it. A saved sound sheet's playOn: is these two sends."
Sound class >> effect: params slot: n [ <stprim: stGpEffect> ]
Sound class >> playSlot: n [ <stprim: stGpPlaySlot> ]
Tune >> primPlayTune: s [ <stprim: stGpPlayTune> ]

"The HUD text overlay — NEW here, not part of MACVM's corpus (its pane had
 seven-segment digits and drew every letter as an empty box, so its GamePane
 never offered text at all). This pane carries a real 5x7 ASCII atlas over the
 indexed layer, in RGB rather than palette indices; `scale` blocks each font
 pixel, so one font covers a HUD and a title screen. The overlay is RETAINED
 between frames: a changing score wants textClear first, or each frame prints
 over the last until the digits are solid blocks."
GamePane >> textClear [ <stprim: stGpTextClear> ]
GamePane >> text: aString x: x y: y r: r g: g b: b scale: k [
    <stprim: stGpText>
]
GamePane >> text: aString x: x y: y r: r g: g b: b [
    ^self text: aString x: x y: y r: r g: g b: b scale: 1
]

"The shader background (layer 0) and multi-frame sprites — also NEW here. A
 Smalltalk game can now compile its own MSL for the bottom layer and animate a
 sprite from frames held on its definition, which is what world/49's twelve
 cosmos backdrops and its flapping fleet need."
GamePane >> shader: mslSource [ <stprim: stGpShader> ]
GamePane >> shaderParam: index value: aNumber [ <stprim: stGpShaderParam> ]
GamePane >> primAddFrame: id rows: hexRows [ <stprim: stGpAddFrame> ]
GamePane >> primPlace: id x: x y: y frame: f [ <stprim: stGpPlaceFrame> ]
GamePane >> primHide: id [ <stprim: stGpHide> ]

Sprite >> addFrame: hexRows [
    "Add another frame of art to my definition (same size as the first).
     Answer myself, so frames can be chained onto a fresh sprite."
    pane primAddFrame: id rows: hexRows.
    ^self
]
Sprite >> moveTo: x y: y frame: f [
    "Move me and pick which frame of my definition to show."
    pane primPlace: id x: x y: y frame: f.
    ^self
]
Sprite >> hide [
    "Stop drawing me until the next moveTo:."
    pane primHide: id.
    ^self
]

"The UFO warble — preset 10, beyond MACVM's ten. Two sines a few Hz apart beat
 against each other; the beat IS the wah, so the pulse rate is the detune, not
 a tremolo. Loop it while a saucer is on screen (it runs 0.9s)."
Sound class >> saucer [ ^self preset: 10 ]

"The capture boss's tractor hum — the same beating trick as the saucer, an
 octave and a half down and slower (110 Hz, 4 Hz apart)."
Sound class >> bossHum [ ^self preset: 11 ]

"The PER-SCANLINE palette. Indices 1..15 can hold a different colour on every
 line of the pane, so a shape drawn ONCE in one of them changes colour down the
 screen and animates by rewriting the line colours — no pixel redrawn. This is
 the copper-bar mechanism, and it is how world/49's tractor beam flows."
GamePane >> linePaletteAt: line index: i r: r g: g b: b [ <stprim: stGpLinePal> ]
